content.thinking:
This is extremely revealing. Looking at the test `clifftop_retreat_enters_untapped_with_mountain` (and similarly `woodland_cemetery_enters_untapped_with_swamp`):

```rust
let mtn = state.create_object(mountain_id, P0, Zone::Battlefield, None, None);
state.get_object_mut(mtn).unwrap().name = "Mountain".into();
state.get_object_mut(mtn).unwrap().subtypes = vec!["Mountain".into()];
```

The test manually sets `obj.subtypes = vec!["Mountain".into()]`. This is how the tests work around the bug - they manually set `subtypes` on the object.


But in a real game, when you play a land via `PlayLand`, the `create_object` call doesn't populate `obj.subtypes` from the card data. So when you play a Swamp or Plains to the battlefield, the object ends up with an empty `subtypes` array throughout its entire lifecycle - from creation in the library, through the hand, and onto the battlefield. This means the `controller_has` check never finds the matching subtype.

Looking at `setup_game` more carefully, I can see it's populating `obj.keywords`, `obj.card_types`, `obj.colors`, and `obj.name` from the card data, but it's completely skipping `obj.subtypes`. So basic lands will always have empty subtypes on the object itself, even though the subtype information exists in the registry. This confirms why `controller_has_matching_land` fails - it's only checking the object's subtypes, not the registry.

The fix would need to check both the object's subtypes and fall back to the registry like `check_condition` does, but the function signature shows `_registry` with an underscore prefix, meaning it's not actually being used. So even though the registry is passed in, there's no way to access the card data to look up the real subtypes. I need to check what `spell_in_hand` actually does and whether it populates the subtypes field on card objects, since that's the root cause of why the tests pass but real gameplay would fail.

